A view that can take text gets an input context, and the key path says where it stopped.

-[NSView inputContext] was NSUnimplementedMethod and answered nil for every view. Nil is the right
answer only for a view that cannot be a text input client, and answering it for one that can is
silent: an application that asks for the context and hands it the event gets nothing back and
nothing logged. iTerm2 3.5 does exactly that. So a context is now made on demand for any view that
implements insertText:replacementRange: or insertText:, which is how macOS decides it, and nil for
the rest. An associated object rather than an ivar, because that is the idiom this port already
uses for per view state.

THE TRACES, all under the existing CIDER_TRACE_KEYS switch and all worth keeping, because they are
what turned this from a guess into a measurement:
  - NSView names the view it makes a context for
  - NSTextInputContext names every handleEvent: ON ITS FIRST LINE, before the guards. A trace after
    an early return cannot tell a call that bailed from a call that never happened, and reading one
    as the other cost task #239 a wrong conclusion twice in one day.
  - NSResponder names which branch interpretKeyEvents: takes, the key binding or the insert, and
    what string reaches insertText:

What they measured for iTerm2 3.5.14: the character reaches -[PTYTextView insertText:] correctly on
every key, handleEvent: is never called on the context it asked for, and -[PTYSession writeTask:] is
never sent. The remaining gap is inside the application.

Task #239.

--- a/AppKit/NSView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSView.m
@@ -2337,9 +2337,38 @@
